-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Diverging Rays:** Perspective rays diverge, making uniform sampling difficult. The scale factor handles this, but if not implemented correctly, artifacts may appear [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The many-to-one mapping from voxels to pixels complicates control flow and requires extra time for resampling weight computation, making it slower than parallel Shear-Warp [S2, S3].
-*   **Resolution Choice:** The resolution of the intermediate image can be chosen to trade off accuracy for speed, allowing for coarser rendering during interaction [S3].
+### Parameters and Edge Cases
+-   **Scale Factor**: Must be chosen such that the closest slice is scaled by 1.0 to prevent enlargement [S2].
+-   **Filter Choice**: Box reconstruction and box low-pass filters are appropriate for decimation and unity scaling [S2].
+-   **Performance**: The many-to-one mapping from voxels to pixels complicates control flow and increases computation time compared to parallel projection [S2].
+-   **Resolution**: The resolution of the intermediate image can be adjusted to provide a coarser, faster rendering during interaction [S3].
 
